  "result": "I wrote the revised node record to `record.jsonl`. It fixes the literal error the gate reported, but it also files a gap on `s1` that will block the law, because even a fixed rule could never be found violated.\n\n**Literal error (the `BARE_LITERAL` verdict).** The civil-time field gives the time of day as a minute of day, a count with no unit (`env.civil_time`'s `minute_of_day`). I now declare the hours on `s6` in that same unit:\n- \"one a.m.\" is minute of day 60.\n- \"six a.m.\" is minute of day 360.\n\nThe rule can compare the minute of day with these directly, so the 60 s conversion factor the coder had to add is no longer needed. The applicability and obligation text now say the same. One thing I couldn't confirm: I declared the unit as `\"1\"`, taken from the ontology's unit table. I don't know the exact unit spelling the gate expects for a count, so the gate may still object to that string.\n\n**Gap on `s1` (\"Except for a motor vehicle\").** The coder found this second problem. The only class that can hold a vehicle that is not self-propelled is `ru.trailer_or_combination`. Its contract, \"vehicle drawn by another, or the combination\", also takes in a trailer still coupled to its towing vehicle, which is self-propelled. So the run's reading correctly leaves that class unresolved under the motor-vehicle definition, and every realistic vehicle then makes the exception either true or unknown. The `forbid` scenario (a vehicle that should be found violated) can't come out that way, and the rule can never find a violation.\n\nI kept the span mapped to `ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle` and recorded an atom gap. The gap asks for a class or attribute that separates an uncoupled trailer, or an animal-drawn vehicle, from a combination. That makes the record uncodable until the operator decides the gap. I didn't swap in another class to get around it. A taxicab or official vehicle alone would come out \"not a motor vehicle\", but only because the scene would be false.\n\nThe other mappings, parameters and scenarios are unchanged, except that the scenario narratives now say \"uncoupled trailer\".",
